---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import InnerOverlays from '../components/InnerOverlays.astro';
import Bars from '../components/work/Bars.astro';
import { naijaDataFacts, neiFacts, neiFindings } from '../data/pages';
---

<Base
  title="Research & data"
  description="Data work by Akanji Oluwaseun Gabriel: Nigeria's economic and labour-market indicators, the naija-data reference datasets, and national survey work with the NBS, FAO, UNICEF and UNODC."
>
  <main id="main" class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">home</a> / <span aria-current="page">research</span>
      </nav>
      <header class="page-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Research &amp; data</span>
        <h1>Numbers you can check.</h1>
        <p>
          I spend my working week on official statistics, and my own time on data products. Everything below has a
          source: a pipeline you can run, a dataset with a citation per row, or a national survey.
        </p>
      </header>

      <article class="case">
        <section class="v-sec">
          <h2 class="v-h">
            <span>Nigeria Economic &amp; Labour Market Intelligence</span>
            <small>01</small>
          </h2>
          <p class="v-why">
            A reproducible pipeline that pulls Nigeria's economic and labour indicators into one database, checks them,
            and produces a findings report. Built in Python and R on PostgreSQL, in Docker, with a migration ledger and
            a revision audit so that a number can never change quietly.
          </p>
          <div class="v-facts">
            {neiFacts.map(([v, l]) => (
              <div>
                <b>{v}</b>
                <span>{l}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <h3 class="h4 cv-sub">What the data says</h3>
          <div class="find">
            {neiFindings.map(([title, text], i) => (
              <article>
                <span>{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <h4 class="h4">{title}</h4>
                <p>{text}</p>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="charts">
            <Bars
              title="Real GDP per person"
              note="Index, 2015 peak = 100"
              rows={[
                ['2015 peak', 100],
                ['Latest', 91.6],
              ]}
              unit="% of peak"
              max={100}
            />
            <Bars
              title="Manufacturing share of GDP"
              note="Then and now"
              rows={[
                ['At its peak', 19.5],
                ['Latest', 8.3],
              ]}
              unit="%"
              max={20}
            />
            <Bars
              title="Work, two ways of counting"
              note="The headline rate next to how the work is actually paid"
              rows={[
                ['Unemployment rate', 3.1],
                ['Vulnerable employment', 66.5],
              ]}
              unit="%"
              max={100}
            />
          </div>
          <p class="art-note">
            Figures from the project's own findings report. The full case study is at{' '}
            <a class="linkish" href="/work/nei/">
              Nigeria Economic Intelligence
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="v-sec">
          <h2 class="v-h">
            <span>naija-data: reference data with sources</span>
            <small>02</small>
          </h2>
          <p class="v-why">
            Nigerian developers kept re-typing the same lists — states, local governments, wards, bank codes, phone
            rules — and each copy was wrong in a different way. naija-data publishes them once, with a source for every
            row, and gives the same answers in Python, JavaScript and Go. 13,392 shared test cases run against all
            three, so the libraries cannot drift apart.
          </p>
          <div class="v-facts">
            {naijaDataFacts.map(([v, l]) => (
              <div>
                <b>{v}</b>
                <span>{l}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="charts">
            <Bars
              title="What is in the dataset"
              note="Rows by type (log scale, so small and large fit together)"
              rows={[
                ['States', 37],
                ['Bank codes', 899],
                ['Local governments', 774],
                ['Wards', 8809],
              ]}
              log
            />
          </div>
          <div class="v-ctas">
            <a class="btn gold" href="/work/naijadata/">
              Read the case study
            </a>
            <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/asgatcreation/naija-data" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              Code on GitHub ↗
            </a>
          </div>
          <p class="art-note">Code is MIT; the data is published under CC BY 4.0.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="v-sec">
          <h2 class="v-h">
            <span>National survey work</span>
            <small>03</small>
          </h2>
          <p>
            At the National Bureau of Statistics I work on surveys that feed Nigeria's official statistics. My part is
            the field and the data: training enumerators, supervising collection, and checking what comes back.
          </p>
          <ul class="feat grid2">
            <li>Crop Cutting Pilot Survey, NBS × FAO — Team Lead, 2026</li>
            <li>Multiple Indicator Cluster Survey (MICS 7), NBS × UNICEF — Assistant Team Supervisor, 2025–26</li>
            <li>Integrated Price and Crop Cutting Survey (IPCCS) 2024 — Supervising Field Officer</li>
            <li>Public Integrity Survey, UNODC × NBS, 2023 — Team Lead Field Officer</li>
            <li>Quarterly GDP and monthly CPI surveys — Field Officer</li>
            <li>National Agricultural Sample Census 2022 and National Business Sample Census 2020 — listing</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="art-note">
            Survey microdata belongs to the NBS and its partners, so none of it appears on this site. What I can offer
            is the method and the experience.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="v-sec">
          <h2 class="v-h">
            <span>Mathematics &amp; statistics</span>
            <small>04</small>
          </h2>
          <p>My own academic work, both at the University of Ibadan:</p>
          <div class="tl">
            <ol>
              <li>
                <span class="when">In progress · MSc Statistics</span>
                <b>Modified Three-Parameter Rama Distribution and Its Applications</b>
                <p>
                  Extending a lifetime distribution with a third parameter and testing how well it fits real datasets
                  against the alternatives.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="when">2021 · MSc Mathematics</span>
                <b>Compact Lie Groups and the Fourier–Stieltjes Transform of Vector Measures</b>
                <p>Harmonic analysis on compact groups, written and typeset in LaTeX.</p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <p class="art-note">
            This is also where the engineering habits come from: if a result cannot be reproduced, it is not a result.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="v-sec">
          <h2 class="v-h">
            <span>Need data work?</span>
            <small>05</small>
          </h2>
          <p>
            Survey design, analysis, forecasting, a reproducible pipeline, or a report your board can read: tell me what
            question you need answered.
          </p>
          <div class="v-ctas">
            <a class="btn gold" href="/#contact">
              Start a conversation
            </a>
            <a class="btn" href="/services/">
              Data services
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
  <InnerOverlays slot="overlays" />
  <script src="../scripts/site.js"></script>
</Base>
